@meta
id: 08-18
title: Where p puts things, and why
chapter: 08
type: lesson
skills: delete-d, put-p
par: 23
minutes: 6
vscode: two drags, two cuts, two clicks, two pastes
open: webhook.config.ts

@brief
Everything in this chapter comes together in one rule, and it is the rule
that makes people distrust paste in vim until somebody explains it.

The register remembers *how* text was taken, not just what was taken.
Take whole lines — `dd`, `3dd`, `yy` — and the register is linewise: `p`
opens a new line below the cursor and drops them in, whatever column you
are standing in. Take part of a line — `x`, `D`, `C` — and the register
is charwise: `p` inserts it into the current line, immediately after the
cursor, creating no new line at all.

Same key, two behaviours, decided entirely by the delete that filled the
register. Nothing you press at paste time changes it.

Two fixes here, one of each kind.

A merge left the timeout comment attached to `retries`. It describes the
timeout. Move it onto the end of the `timeoutMs` line — the text lands
inside that line.

The dead letter queue belongs with the retry settings, not after the
secret. Move that whole line up so it sits directly under `retries` — the
text lands as its own line.

Do both without retyping either piece.

@keys
| `D` | delete to the end of the line — charwise |
| `dd` | delete the whole line — linewise |
| `p` | put: inline after the cursor, or as a new line below |
| `$` | end of the line |
| `f/` `h` | forward to the slash, then one back |

@hints
Do the comment first. There is one register, and the second delete will
overwrite whatever the first one left in it.
--
For the comment, start on the space *before* the `//` so the space
travels with it — then `D`, and put it at the `$` of the timeout line.
For the line move, `dd` and `p` are already familiar; the only question
is which line you are standing on when you put.
--
`j f/ h D` lifts the comment. `j $ p` drops it on the end of the next
line. Then `2j dd` lifts the queue line, `3k` gets you to `retries`, and
`p` puts it underneath.

@solution
    j f/h D  j $ p  2j dd  3k p  :wq<Enter>

Fifteen keystrokes, two moves, two completely different results from the
same `p`.

Watch what the charwise put did with the cursor: `p` placed the text
*after* the character you were on, which is why you went to `$` first. If
you want it before the cursor instead, that is `P` — and on a linewise
register `P` means above rather than below. One pair of keys, the same
meaning in both shapes: `p` is after, `P` is before.

This is the answer to the question every VS Code refugee eventually asks:
"why did my paste land on its own line when I wanted it inside the line?"
Because you filled the register with `dd`. If you want charwise text, take
it charwise — `D`, `x`, or in the next chapter `dw` and `d$`. There is a
way to force a linewise register to paste inline, and it is ugly enough
that nobody uses it. Take the text the shape you want it.

@start webhook.config.ts
export const webhookConfig = {
  retries: 5, // keep this under the gateway's own budget
  timeoutMs: 2000,
  secretEnv: 'WEBHOOK_SECRET',
  deadLetterQueue: 'webhook-dlq',
};

@expected webhook.config.ts
export const webhookConfig = {
  retries: 5,
  deadLetterQueue: 'webhook-dlq',
  timeoutMs: 2000, // keep this under the gateway's own budget
  secretEnv: 'WEBHOOK_SECRET',
};
